From 0537c3284d47364a68fb881270011b56ee72aec3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PyScotch <pyscotch@noreply.invalid>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:44:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 236/240] Bugfix: make `meshCoarsen()` report when the mesh
 cannot be coarsened [report R. Perry]

`meshCoarsen()` ignored its `coarnbr` and `coarrat` parameters and
always returned 0, so the multilevel mesh separation recursed until
the node count dropped below its threshold, which never happens for a
mesh that cannot shrink: the default `SCOTCH_meshOrder()` strategy
overflowed the stack on a mesh of 300 disjoint triangles. Also, when
the matching routine could not allocate its hash table, it silently
reported no coarsening and left the multinode array uninitialized.

Apply the same contract as `graphCoarsen()`: return 1 when the mesh is
already small enough or when the number of coarse elements is not
below the contraction ratio, and 2 when the matching routine fails.
Check the result of the final re-allocation, and free the work arrays
on all error paths. Move the node threshold test from
`vmeshSeparateMlCoarsen()` into `meshCoarsen()`.

Add a mesh that cannot be coarsened to `test_scotch_mesh_order`.

Refs: SEP-03
